WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.833
(gentle upbeat music)

00:00:00.833 --> 00:00:02.730
- [Narrator] A debate over
how the government manages

00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:05.510
its budget is taking over Capitol hill.

00:00:05.510 --> 00:00:06.490
The keyword is...

00:00:06.490 --> 00:00:07.332
- Reconciliation.

00:00:07.332 --> 00:00:08.211
- Reconciliation.

00:00:08.211 --> 00:00:09.044
- I am willing to go to reconciliation.

00:00:10.250 --> 00:00:12.250
- [Narrator] Budget
reconciliation is a process

00:00:12.250 --> 00:00:14.230
that speeds up lawmaking.

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:16.750
That may come in handy for
Washington's majority party

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:17.970
this summer.

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:20.240
While bipartisan group
reached an agreement

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:23.890
to spend more than $1 trillion
on infrastructure in June.

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:24.960
- This is roads and bridges,

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:27.740
but also lots of other
kinds of infrastructure.

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:29.240
- [Narrator] Democrats also want to pass

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:30.700
the American Families Plan

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:32.870
to shore up the country's
social safety net.

00:00:32.870 --> 00:00:35.980
- Republicans and this
group did not wanna go along

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:38.440
with my and my family plan issues

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:40.250
and childcare tax credits,

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:42.800
the human infrastructure I talk about.

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:44.477
- [Narrator] In total, the
president wants to spend

00:00:44.477 --> 00:00:47.453
$6 trillion in fiscal year 2022.

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:51.070
In order to get anywhere close
to that level of spending,

00:00:51.070 --> 00:00:54.200
Democrats will need to
use budget reconciliation.

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:56.480
But there's no guarantee
that this will work.

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:00.190
Divisions within the party could
scuttle the entire process.

00:01:00.190 --> 00:01:02.000
Here's how budget reconciliation works

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:05.113
and why Democrats are using
it to pass Biden's agenda.

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:08.740
Every fiscal year,
Congress has an opportunity

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:10.680
to pass a new budget bill.

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:13.970
This is an opening to pass
laws with a simple majority,

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:16.830
without needing 60 votes
to overcome a filibuster.

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:18.917
- Let's be clear purpose
of this in many ways,

00:01:18.917 --> 00:01:22.490
from a partisan perspective is

00:01:22.490 --> 00:01:26.410
to get around the 60 vote
hurdle on regular legislation.

00:01:26.410 --> 00:01:27.650
- [Narrator] Bill Hoagland has worked on

00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:30.220
congressional budgets for
more than three decades.

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:32.040
- There had been something like about

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:35.570
22 reconciliation bills enacted into law,

00:01:35.570 --> 00:01:40.350
and I was involved in, but 18 of those.

00:01:40.350 --> 00:01:42.763
- [Narrator] Here's how the
reconciliation process works.

00:01:42.763 --> 00:01:43.614
(words slide in)

00:01:43.614 --> 00:01:44.447
(gentle jazzy music)

00:01:44.447 --> 00:01:47.010
When a party controls the
House, Senate and White House,

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:49.550
it can write the government's budget.

00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:53.280
The first draft of the budget
is called a Budget Resolution.

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:56.400
This document can include
reconciliation instructions

00:01:56.400 --> 00:01:59.210
for one or more committees
to change existing rules

00:01:59.210 --> 00:02:02.090
on spending revenue or debt.

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:04.390
These instructions allow
the controlling party

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:06.290
to fold its goals into the budget,

00:02:06.290 --> 00:02:08.460
instead of passing them as regular laws

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:10.060
that are subject to filibusters.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:12.930
Once a budget resolution passes,

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:14.680
the next step is committee review,

00:02:14.680 --> 00:02:16.780
where members of Congress write policies

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:20.340
that deliver what the
reconciliation instructions ask for.

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:22.320
For example, the Transportation Committee

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:25.520
may receive instructions that
affect road building programs.

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:27.550
These instructions can ask the committee

00:02:27.550 --> 00:02:29.470
to change how much is spent,

00:02:29.470 --> 00:02:31.140
how much tax is collected,

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:34.490
or how much debt the country
takes on for this program.

00:02:34.490 --> 00:02:38.000
But new policies sometimes
depend on rules and regulations

00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:40.180
that don't directly affect the budget

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:42.450
and might not pass in reconciliation.

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:44.890
- It's not out of the realm of possibility

00:02:44.890 --> 00:02:47.660
that you couldn't create new spending

00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:51.820
or new taxes or new provisions,

00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:55.400
but it has to have a direct fiscal impact.

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:57.480
- [Narrator] Once the
recommendations are finalized,

00:02:57.480 --> 00:02:59.970
they're sent to the
Senate Budget Committee.

00:02:59.970 --> 00:03:02.150
This group verifies that the revised plan

00:03:02.150 --> 00:03:03.280
changes the budget in a way

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:05.860
that follows the rules of Congress.

00:03:05.860 --> 00:03:07.550
After passing committee review,

00:03:07.550 --> 00:03:09.430
the budget is sent to the Senate chamber

00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:11.393
for final consideration on the floor.

00:03:12.250 --> 00:03:15.200
A budget reconciliation bill
carries special privileges,

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:16.833
which can speed up its passage.

00:03:17.940 --> 00:03:20.030
For starters, there's a 20 hour time limit

00:03:20.030 --> 00:03:22.480
to review the proposed changes.

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:24.310
In the normal lawmaking process,

00:03:24.310 --> 00:03:27.280
debate is what makes
Capitol Hill stall out.

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:29.760
Often in the form of a filibuster.

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:32.160
A reconciliation bill
avoids the filibuster

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:34.620
and can pass with a simple majority.

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:35.720
In the event of a tie,

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:38.190
which is possible in today's split Senate.

00:03:38.190 --> 00:03:42.330
The Vice-President steps in
to cast a tie-breaking vote.

00:03:42.330 --> 00:03:44.730
If the vote passes both
chambers of Congress,

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:47.810
the reconciliation bill, then
heads to the president's desk

00:03:47.810 --> 00:03:49.423
where it can be signed into law.

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:53.970
Making rules in this
manner comes with caveats.

00:03:53.970 --> 00:03:56.490
For instance, reconciliation
measures are supposed

00:03:56.490 --> 00:03:59.790
to focus on the budget
impacts and little else.

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:01.900
This is one of several restrictions

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:04.630
put in place by the Senate's Byrd rule.

00:04:04.630 --> 00:04:07.160
- The biggest one is a provision says,

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:10.630
does the provision in reconciliation,

00:04:10.630 --> 00:04:14.020
is it merely incidental
to an overall policy?

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:15.480
If it doesn't pass that test,

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:18.530
then any United States
Senator can stand up and say,

00:04:18.530 --> 00:04:23.120
Mr. President, that provision
XYZ violates the Byrd rule.

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:25.692
I raise at budget at point of order,

00:04:25.692 --> 00:04:30.500
and unless there's 60 votes
to waive that point of order,

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:32.910
that particular provision
is surgically removed.

00:04:32.910 --> 00:04:34.600
Doesn't kill the bill.

00:04:34.600 --> 00:04:36.150
Just simply takes it out.

00:04:36.150 --> 00:04:38.890
- [Narrator] Early in 2021,
the Senate parliamentarian's

00:04:38.890 --> 00:04:40.700
interpretation of the Byrd rule,

00:04:40.700 --> 00:04:44.590
forced Democrats to abandon
a piece of the COVID-19 bill.

00:04:44.590 --> 00:04:47.470
- On this reconciliation
bill does not include

00:04:47.470 --> 00:04:50.830
an increase in the minimum
wage to $15 an hour.

00:04:50.830 --> 00:04:52.570
In my view it should have.

00:04:52.570 --> 00:04:54.230
- [Narrator] Democrats
are on a tight timeline

00:04:54.230 --> 00:04:56.410
to pass laws that affect many areas,

00:04:56.410 --> 00:04:59.690
including infrastructure,
climate change, taxes,

00:04:59.690 --> 00:05:02.270
paid sick leave, unemployment payments,

00:05:02.270 --> 00:05:04.940
childcare and education programs.

00:05:04.940 --> 00:05:05.920
It's a lot to get done

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:09.140
before the 2022 campaign cycle kicks off.

00:05:09.140 --> 00:05:10.540
- When we get into next year

00:05:11.750 --> 00:05:14.790
fiscal year 20 budget resolution 23,

00:05:14.790 --> 00:05:16.930
then it becomes so much more partisan

00:05:16.930 --> 00:05:20.836
that it may even more difficult
to achieve agreements.

00:05:20.836 --> 00:05:21.669
(gentle somber music)

00:05:21.669 --> 00:05:23.670
- [Narrator] In order to
use budget reconciliation

00:05:23.670 --> 00:05:25.310
in an evenly split Senate.

00:05:25.310 --> 00:05:28.278
Democrats need everyone in
the party to fall in line.

00:05:28.278 --> 00:05:31.710
That puts all eyes on moderate
Democrats like Joe Manchin,

00:05:31.710 --> 00:05:33.770
who could vote no on a reconciliation bill

00:05:33.770 --> 00:05:36.040
and prevent any laws from passing.

00:05:36.040 --> 00:05:37.830
- If they think in reconciliation,

00:05:37.830 --> 00:05:39.820
I'm gonna throw caution to the wind

00:05:39.820 --> 00:05:41.790
and go to 5 or $6 trillion

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:46.160
when we can only afford one or
one and a half or maybe two,

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:47.940
and what we can pay for it.

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:49.343
Then I can't be there.

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:51.960
- [Narrator] With the
spending figures piling up

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:53.420
and time running out,

00:05:53.420 --> 00:05:55.760
this year's budget talks
are shaping up to be

00:05:55.760 --> 00:05:58.687
a particularly high stakes negotiation.

00:05:58.687 --> 00:06:02.687
(gentle somber music continues)

